findings.recommendation:
Owner: b2-domain (backend-engineer), src/domain/planner.js plus tests. A c2 follow-up applies in either case.
Resolution, preferred: plan() returns the exact optimum (same objective, rejections, candidate codes and relaxations) quickly on the API-reachable envelope:
- up to 12 offers;
- availability up to 100,000;
- quantities up to 100,000;
- maxPickups up to 5;
- any budget or deadline.
For example, enumerate supplier subsets and solve each subset's minimum-cost cover by bounded dynamic programming over bundle counts, instead of a DFS over every multiplicity.
Tests to add: a named timing test (e.g. 'SEC-B-1: stock 100 per supplier, 10,000 cups -> feasible A, under 50 ms'), the oracle property test extended to larger availability on small catalogs, and sec-phb-planner-sweep.mjs and tr-phb-checks.mjs both exiting 0.
Alternative: a design change agreed through the orchestrator that caps onHand and quantities to an envelope that a recorded sweep proves cannot reach PLANNER_LIMIT, with c2 mapping PLANNER_LIMIT to a typed 422 (FU-1).
In either case, c2 adds a per-customer rate limit on POST /api/requests/:id/plan (a new follow-up in followups.md).
